1 NOTE {geni:about_me} Claes Netherlands, Archival Indexes, Vital Records, 1600-2000 Name:	 Claes Event Type:	 Baptism Event Date:	 01 Jun 1597 Event Place:	 Dordrecht, Zuid-Holland, Nederland Event Place (Original):	 Dordrecht Father's 
2 CONC Name:	 Jan Willemsz de Witt Mother's Name:	 Jaquemijne .... Affiliate Image Identifier:	 /show.php?archive=rad&identifier=FF13163D-3A7D-4854-AD7B-DF123B70E9CB Nederland, Indexen van de Archieven, Primaire Archiefstukken (BS en DTB
2 CONC ), 1600-2000," database, FamilySearch (https://familysearch.org/ark:/61903/1:1:QLDJ-SFG2 : 23 August 2017), Claes, Baptism 01 Jun 1597, Dordrecht, Zuid-Holland, Nederland; from database, openarchives (https://www.openarch.nl : 201
2 CONC 6); citing Doopakten Dordrecht, archive 1572-1702-WIL-YVA, inventory number 1-7, folio; Doopakten Dordrecht; Regionaal Archief Dordrecht.
2 CONT
2 CONT Accompanied Hendrick Hudson to America on the "Half Moon" in 1609, which "discovered" and subsequently named the Hudson River. [DeWitt 5-99.FTW]
2 CONT
2 CONT ---
2 CONT
2 CONT ===Origins 
2 CONT
2 CONT From https://www.wikitree.com/wiki/De_Witt-107
2 CONT
2 CONT Son of Son of Johan Willemsz de Wit and Jaepken Jansdr van Baresteijn
2 CONT
2 CONT Claes Jansz de Witt was baptized on 1 June 1597 in Dordrecht, the Netherlands [1] or was he the Witte Claß from Germany, as it was spelled in the church book of Esens at the birth of the illegitimate Tiade in 1647, whose father wa
2 CONC s said to be Tiark Claeßen, son of Witte Claß.[2]
2 CONT
2 CONT ----
2 CONT
2 CONT Claes Nicholas DeWITT b. abt 1594, Holland, Netherlands, m. abt 1618, Taatje VAN LUEVEN, b. abt 1596. Claes died Esens?, East Friesland, Ger. He lived at Grootholdt, Zunderlandt, Holland, the most southerly of the three natural di
2 CONC visions of the old Dutchy of Westphalia, consisting of hills and vales, woods and meadows, suited for grazing and dairying.
2 CONT
2 CONT Children: 
2 CONT *1. Jan DEWITT
2 CONT *2. Emmerentie DEWITT
2 CONT *3. Tjerck Claessen DEWITT b: 1620 in Grootholt, Sunderland, Holland, Netherlands
2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas discovered New York (New Amsterdam) on a trip with Hendrick Hudson on the ship the 'Half Moon' in 1609. They had three children. Nicholas had the title of Doctor.
2 CONT
2 CONT ----------
2 CONT
2 CONT Ben M. Angel notes: Given the importance of the Dutch "discovery" of Manhattan Island, it's rather surprising to find so little official documentation on the event. Mostly, the supposed discovery by the 15-year-old (at most) Nicho
2 CONC las de Witt is passed along by what appears to be word-of-web.
2 CONT
2 CONT One would have to presume that his doctorate was achieved after the event. He doesn't figure prominently in any of the Wikipedia articles (in any language) on the 1609 voyage of the Halve Maen under Henry Hudson.
2 CONT
2 CONT This is what is given in the English Wikipedia article (which differs little from the Dutch, German, and French articles):
2 CONT
2 CONT In 1609, Hudson was chosen by the Dutch East India Company to find an easterly passage to Asia.[19] He was told to sail through the Arctic Ocean north of Russia, into the Pacific and so to the Far East. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Hudson departed Amsterdam on April 4 in command of the Dutch ship Halve Maen.[20] He could not complete the specified route because ice blocked the passage, as with all previous such voyages, and he turned the ship around in mid-M
2 CONC ay while somewhere east of Norway's North Cape. At that point, acting entirely outside his instructions, Hudson pointed the ship west to try to find a passage in that direction.[21]
2 CONT
2 CONT Having heard rumors of a passage to the Pacific, by way of John Smith of Jamestown and Samuel de Champlain, Hudson and his crew decided to try to seek a westerly passage through North America. The Native Americans who gave the inf
2 CONC ormation to Smith and Champlain were likely referring to what are known today as the Great Lakes (and which could not be reached via any navigable waterways).
2 CONT
2 CONT They reached the Grand Banks, south of Newfoundland, on July 2, and in mid-July made landfall near what is now LaHave, Nova Scotia.[22] Here they encountered Native Americans who were accustomed to trading with the French; they we
2 CONC re willing to trade beaver pelts, but apparently no trades occurred.[23] The ship stayed in the area about ten days, the crew replacing a broken mast and fishing for food. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On the 25th a dozen men from the Halve Maen, using muskets and small cannon, went ashore and assaulted the village near their anchorage. They drove the people from the settlement and took their boat and other property (probably pe
2 CONC lts and trade goods).[24]
2 CONT
2 CONT On August 4 the ship was at Cape Cod, from which Hudson sailed south to the entrance of the Chesapeake Bay. Rather than entering the Chesapeake he explored the coast to the north, finding Delaware Bay but continuing on north. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 3 he reached the estuary of the river that initially was called the "North River" or "Mauritius" and now carries his name. He was not the first to discover the estuary, though, as it had been known since the voyage of
2 CONC  Giovanni da Verrazzano in 1524. 
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 6, 1609 John Colman of his crew was killed by Indians with an arrow to his neck.[25] Hudson sailed into the upper bay on September 11,[26] and the following day began a journey up what is now known as the Hudson River
2 CONC [27] Over the next ten days his ship ascended the river, reaching a point about where the present-day capital of Albany is located.[28]
2 CONT
2 CONT On September 23, Hudson decided to return to Europe. He put in at Dartmouth on November 7, and was detained by authorities who wanted access to his log. He managed to pass the log to the Dutch ambassador to England, who sent it, a
2 CONC long with his report, to Amsterdam.[29]
2 CONT
2 CONT While exploring the river, Hudson had traded with several native groups, mainly obtaining furs. His voyage was used to establish Dutch claims to the region and to the fur trade that prospered there when a trading post was establis
2 CONC hed at Albany in 1614. New Amsterdam on Manhattan Island became the capital of New Netherland in 1625.
2 CONT
2 CONT References:
2 CONT
2 CONT 19.^ Willard Sterne Randall "First Encounters," American Heritage, Spring 2009.
2 CONT
2 CONT 20.^ Hunter, Douglas (2009). Half Moon: Henry Hudson and the voyage that redrew the map of the New World. Bloomsbury Press. ISBN 1-59691-680-X, p. 11.
2 CONT
2 CONT 21.^ Hunter (2009), p. 56-7.
2 CONT
2 CONT 22.^ Hunter (2009), p. 92-4.
2 CONT
2 CONT 23.^ Hunter (2009), p. 98, and Juet (1609), July 19th entry.
2 CONT
2 CONT 24.^ Hunter (2009), p. 102-105, and Juet (1609), July 25th entry.
2 CONT
2 CONT 25.^ Roberts, Sam (September 4, 2009). "New York’s Coldest Case: A Murder 400 Years Old". New York Times. Retrieved 2009-09-04.
2 CONT
2 CONT 26.^ Nevius, Michelle and James, "New York's many 9/11 anniversaries: the Staten Island Peace Conference", Inside the Apple: A Streetwise History of New York City, 2008-09-08. Retrieved 2009-05-31.
2 CONT
2 CONT 27.^ Juet, Robert (1609), Juet's Journal of Hudson's 1609 Voyage from the 1625 edition of Purchas His Pilgrimes and transcribed 2006 by Brea Barthel, "Juet's Journal of Hudson's 1609 Voyage".
2 CONT
2 CONT 28.^ Hunter (2009), p. 230-5.
2 CONT
2 CONT 29.^ Shorto, Russell (2004). The Island at the Center of the World. Vintage Books. ISBN 1-4000-7867-9, pg.31
2 CONT
2 CONT ---------
2 CONT
2 CONT Nicholas "Claes" de Witt also received no mention in Juit's Journal. These are the entries surrounding 3 September, the day of discovery:
2 CONT
2 CONT The second, in the morning close weather, the wind at south in the morning, from twelve until two of the clock we steered north northwest and had sounding one and twenty fathoms, and in running one glass we had but sixteen fathoms
2 CONC , then seventeen, ans so shoalder and shoalder until it came to twelve fathoms. We saw a great fire, but could not see the land, then we came to ten fathoms, whereupon we brought our tackes aboard, and stood to the easteward east 
2 CONC southeast four glasses. Then the sun rose and we steered away north again and saw the land from the west by north to the northwest by north, all like broken islands, and our soundings were eleven and ten fathoms. Then we loost in 
2 CONC for the shore, and fair by the shore we had seven fathoms. The course along the land we found to be northeast by north. From the land which we had first sight of, until we came to a great lake of water, as we could judge it to be,
2 CONC  being drowned land, which made it rise like islands which was in length ten leagues. The mouth of that lake hath many shoals, and the sea breaketh on them as it is cast out of the mouth of it. And from that lake or bay, the land 
2 CONC lyeth north by east, and we had a great stream out of the bay, and from thence our sounding was 10 fathoms, two leagues from the land. At five of the clock, we anchored, being little wind, and rode in eight fathoms water, the nigh
2 CONC t was fair. This night I found the land to hall the compass 8 degrees. For to the northward of us we saw high hills. For the day before we found not only above 2 degrees of variation. This is very good land to fall with, and a ple
2 CONC asant land to see.
2 CONT
2 CONT The third, the morning misty until 10 of the clock, then it cleared and the wind came to the south southeast, so we weighed and stood to the northward. The land is very pleasant and high, and bold to fall withall. At three of the 
2 CONC clock in the afternoon, we came to three great rivers. We stood along to the northernmost, thinking to have gone into it, but we found it to have a very shoald bar before it, for we had but ten foot water. Then we cast about to th
2 CONC e southward, and found two fathoms, three fathoms, and three and a quarter, til we cam to the souther side of them, then we had five and six fathoms and anchored. So we sent in our boat to sound and they found no less water than f
2 CONC our, five, six and seven fathoms, and returned in an hour and a half. So we weighed and went in, and rode in five fathoms. Ozie ground, and saw many salmons, mullets, and rays very great. The height is 40 degrees, 30 minutes.
2 CONT --------------------
