2 CONT
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT C.Tjerck Claeszen DeWitt (Claes) (1620-2/17/1700) came from Holland in 1650, probably first of his family to come to New Amsterdam, and soon settled in Kingston, NY. Earliest information in “Trouw Boeck” or the register of marriag
2 CONC es of the DRC in city of New Amsterdam where recorded his m. 4/24/1656, Barbara Andrieszen, from Amsterdam, d. 7/6/1714. Res. in New Amsterdam til b. of eldest son, Andries, 1657, when he moved to Miltwyck, remaining there til his
2 CONC  d. The place was well fortified, and he helped defend the village at the time of the Indian attack in 1663. His daughter was taken captive (Jannetje) & recovered. He was one of the magistrates. In 1669 obtained liberty to erect h
2 CONC ouse and barn on his land outside of Wiltwyck. He was a navigator & owned a sloop of 50 ft. keel. Both a trader and farmer with some rank among the original settlers. In 1689 he refused to take the oath of allegiance to the Britis
2 CONC h crown, as did many others, because the British authorities had not fulfilled their treaty stipulations on the country being surrendered to them, and he did not acknowledge that power during his lifetime.
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT In 1639 Tjerck and brother Jan emigrated to America
2 CONT --------------------
2 CONT http://www.mrjumbo.com/contents/genealogy/dewitt/tjerck/   .... < an excellent reference guide 
2 CONT
2 CONT also:
2 CONT
2 CONT http://www.our-genealogy.com/bettes/ancestry-de-witt/tjerck-claessen-de-witt.htm 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tjerck came from a farm near Esens, in Ostfriesland, which today is part of Germany. (Other) mistaken information came from an erroneous transcription of the original handwritten record from 1656. The transcription, which everyone
2 CONC  cites, is also handwritten, but it is not the original. The original is lost today. The erroneous transcription still exists and is being lovingly cared for by able archivists. For pictures of the erroneous transcription, and mor
2 CONC e detailed discussion of the errors and misperceptions that have arisen from this church record, see here.]
2 CONT Presumed buried in Kingston, New York
2 CONT
2 CONT "Tjerck is first recorded in Beverwyck (near Albany) in 1655... by the 1650's the population of that area was larger than that of Manhattan."
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT From find-a-grave:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Tjerck married Barbara Andriessen on April 24, 1656 in the Reformed Collegiate Dutch Church in New Amsterdam. 
2 CONT
2 CONT Tjerck died on February 17, 1700 at Kingston, Ulster County, New York.
2 CONT *Note
2 CONT dicknlissa@comcast.net
2 CONT Their suggestion:
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT Although Tjerck Claessen DeWitt (1630-1700) is almost certainly buried in the Old Dutch Reformed Church churchyard, the headstone shown on Find A Grave is that of his grandson. The oldest existing headstone for the cemetery is tha
2 CONC t of Tjerck's oldest son, Andries, who died tragically in a building accident in 1710. This stone has been removed to the safety of the church museum, and may be viewed there.
2 CONT -------------------------
2 CONT
2 CONT In September, 1660, he exchanged his Albany property with Madame de Hutter for land in Wiltwyck (now Kingston), "possession to be given May 1, 1661." He probably took possession at that time, as in September, 1661, he appears as p
2 CONC laintiff in an action at law before the Schepens Court of Wiltwyck, and on October 11th the same court ordered the Sheriff (Roeleff Swartwout) to pay him three and a half schepels of wheat in eight days and seven more in one month
2 CONC . From this time until death, he resided in Kingston and Hurley, and some of the land which he purchased is still in the hands of his descendants.
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT Commissary of village of Wildwyck (Kingston area) New Netherland
2 CONT
2 CONT source: an account drawn form various sources:
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT
2 CONT "In this attack on the two villages fifteen men, four women and two children were killed. Most of the women and children killed were burned to death. Of the prisoners taken by the Indians at this outbreak there were thirteen women
2 CONC , thirty children, and one man. At Kingston twelve houses were burned, while the New Village was entirely destroyed.
2 CONT
2 CONT Soldiers including PEER Jan Hendricks  were now sent up from New York, and the Indians were hunted from mountain to mountain.  The rescued children included '''Tjerck Claessen en de Witt’s oldest daughter'''. (Mathijs Jansen Van K
2 CONC EULEN’s future daughter-in-law) Peer Jan Hendrick married Mathijs’ daughter Annetje.
2 CONT
2 CONT Peer was a Sargent in the Dutch West India Company’s troops sent to Esopus Jun 1663 under Capt. Martin Krieger in Colonel Cragier’s regiment.  He took part in the rescue of the women and children captured in the Esopus raid on Wil
2 CONC twyck (Kingston), June, 1663.  Two months after the raid, the Indians were engaged at the Esopus and the captives freed, including T'''jaatje and Jannetje DeWitt,''' and Jannaken Van Vliet.   '''Tjaatje Dewitt would be Peer’s futu
2 CONC re sister-in-law by later marrying Matthys Matthyssen''',  Annetje’s brother and progenitor of the Van Keuren family."
2 CONT --------------------
