1 NOTE {geni:about_me} well researched source: http://www.mrjumbo.com/contents/genealogy/dewitt/tette/index.html
2 CONT
2 CONT David Riker's Directory .... says that Jan Albertszen  van Steenwyck married Ida Claeszen DeWitt, the sister of Tjerck  Claeszen DeWitt.
2 CONT
2 CONT Note: Research could be initiated at the Old Dutch Church in Kingston, once known as Wiltwyck, "The founding of Old Dutch Church in 1659 was a result of direct solicitation to Peter Stuyvesant by early settlers of the area, the th
2 CONC ird oldest settlement in New Netherland. The Classis of Amsterdam, Holland, responded by sending a Dominie (Pastor). Local settlers immediately organized the church. This first Dominie was particularly active in the Second Esopus 
2 CONC War of 1663(when Ida and Jan Albertsen were killed), administering to the sick and dying and reporting this incident to the authorities in New Amsterdam and Holland.
2 CONT
2 CONT From its founding, meticulous preparation of baptism and marriage records have been kept, from 1659 to the present. The details included in these records make them a boon to historians and genealogists alike. The church maintains 
2 CONC a museum that houses these records and other artifacts.
2 CONT
2 CONT The Old Dutch Church Heritage Museum archives are available for public research, under the direction of a church historian. If you are interested in geneological research, please contact the Old Dutch Church office at 845 338-6759
2 CONC  to schedule an appointment. At this time, the Church is unable to process genological requests for the public.
2 CONT
2 CONT The present church structure is the third to be sited on the same plot of land inside the Stockade Area of Kingston, as established by Peter Stuyvesant and later confirmed by the first British governor of New York in 1664."
2 CONT
2 CONT another source:
2 CONT
2 CONT Proceedings of the New York State Historical Association with the Quarterly Journal: 2nd-21st Annual Meeting with a List of New Members, Volume 11 (Google eBook)
2 CONT
2 CONT New York State Historical Association The Association, 1912 - New York (State)
2 CONT
2 CONT ~• Tierck Clafsen DeWitt and Descendants of His Son Luycas DeWitt
2 CONT  By Vona DeWitt Smith (see documents)
2 CONT
2 CONT "Tjerck is last recorded in Ostfriesland in 1647. He is first recorded in New Netherlands, as a carter up by Albany (hauling wood for people), in early 1655.
2 CONT
2 CONT Ida and Jan are married, with children, by 1661, in Kingston. Tjerck signs over to Jan a power of attorney, because Jan is going to go over, with Ida, to Europe, soon. Tjerck wants Jan to do some business for him over there. (This
2 CONC  business specifically has to do with the estate of Tjerck and Ida's family.) Tjerck describes Jan as his brother-in-law, so we know that Ida is already in North America, and we know that Jan is already married to her.
2 CONT
2 CONT Someone else gives Jan a similar power of attorney before the same trip. This was pretty typical in the colony: If you know someone who's going to go back over to the Old Country, you send them with letters, notes, cash, legal pow
2 CONC ers to transact business, requests, instructions, etc. For major stuff, you would have it notarized in New Amsterdam before they went, so everyone on the other side would know they were duly empowered.
2 CONT
2 CONT So the return trip on de Vos in 1662 was for both of them a trip back to New Netherlands, not the first time either had left Europe. And yes, I think while Ida was home in Ostfriesland, the family arranged to have Amerens/Emmerent
2 CONC je and Jan Claessen come to North America with her." ~ Doug Bradley
2 CONT --------------------
